Technical Specification: ESP32-C3 Intercom Signal Generator (Calibrator)This document provides a comprehensive technical overview, electrical schematics, and production-ready firmware for the standalone Intercom Signal Generator. This device is designed to simulate a real, dual-polar ($\pm 4\text{V AC}$) intercom call signal on a test bench to safely calibrate and debug custom receivers/handsets.1. System Architecture & Core ConceptsTo simulate a classic analog coordinate intercom call line (e.g., Urmet 1132) on a lab bench, we need to generate an Amplitude Modulated (AM) signal. The target signal consists of:Carrier Frequency (Audio Tone): $800\text{ Hz} - 1500\text{ Hz}$ square/sine wave.Modulation Frequency: $50\text{ Hz}$ (emulating the unfiltered ripple of the building's central AC power supply).Target Amplitude: $\pm 4\text{V AC}$ peak-to-peak.1.1. Single-Supply to Dual-Polar Conversion (AC Coupling)A standard N-channel MOSFET acts as a Low-Side switch, which can only output unipolar pulses from $0\text{V}$ (GND) to $+V_{cc}$ (DC supply voltage).To obtain a true dual-polar $\pm 4\text{V}$ AC signal without utilizing symmetrical dual-rail power supplies, we implement AC Coupling.A series electrolytic capacitor ($C_1$) blocks the DC offset (average voltage of $+4\text{V}$) and allows only the AC component to pass. This shifts the signal's baseline to a virtual zero, generating a clean $\pm 4\text{V}$ waveform relative to GND.2. Principle Electrical Schematic                                        +V_PSU (External supply, +8V or +16V)
